WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.519
It's great to welcome to the program today.

00:00:02.519 --> 00:00:07.080
Nicholas Carr, who's the author of several
books on technology and culture, including

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.280
the Pulitzer Prize finalist, The Shallows
What the Internet is Doing to Our Brains.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.770
So great to have you on.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.120
I really appreciate your time.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.200
Thanks very much, David.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.200
My pleasure.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.640
So to start with, I think there's probably
a lot of people in my audience who generally

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.190
have the sense that maybe over the last many
years it's become a little more difficult

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:30.160
to do, sort of deeper, deeper learning, focusing
on one task for an extended period of time.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.230
There's some vague notion that maybe there's
something about social media in 140 character

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:35.970
posts that may be sort of retraining our brain
to some extent.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.180
But how do you generally characterize the
way that the modern Internet and technology

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.890
have changed the way that we mostly do our
thinking?

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:45.149
I think human beings have trouble concentrating,
have trouble screening out distractions.

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:48.610
In general, we're very distractable and you
really do have to practice that and train

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:49.839
yourself to concentrate and focus.

00:00:49.839 --> 00:00:53.610
And I think what the Internet does in general,
and certainly social media in particular,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:59.199
is it doesn't give us either the opportunity
or the encouragement to train ourselves and

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:02.550
and practice skills of attentiveness because
it just floods us with with information, information

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.380
that's interesting to us and grabs our attention,
but in little chunks.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:08.310
And so my argument is that essentially what
we pay to get this abundance of fast flowing

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.480
information is our ability to concentrate.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.490
We can't tap into those deeper, more attentive
ways of thinking.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.490
Is there something that's adaptive.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.720
From an evolutionary standpoint about being
easily distracted in the sense of you're building

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.720
a fire, but there's a threat off in the distance
and so your mind goes to that shiny object,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.510
they're moving object or something along those
lines?

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.260
I think that's exactly it.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.110
You know, you can you can understand how in
the distant past.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.840
In order to stay alive, in order to survive,
you need to know everything that was going

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.520
on around you, particularly anything new which
might be a threat or might be an opportunity

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.520
to eat or whatever.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.300
So, ah, the way our brains work is that we're
rewarded with dopamine and other ways, little

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.550
pleasure producing chemicals to gather information.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:45.500
And I think it's exactly that, that this is
an evolutionary instinct that unfortunately

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:51.174
we bring over into this new digital environment
where there's no end to new bits of information

00:01:51.174 --> 00:01:52.174
to grab and briefly hold our attention.

00:01:52.174 --> 00:01:56.730
One thing I've read and heard from some folks
is the idea that there was a time in human

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:01.900
history not that long ago, really, where there
would be a big debate over a fact because

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.200
we did not have facts so readily accessible
on our phones.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.999
And that I mean, this is a very sort of utopian
outlook now that the facts don't need to be

00:02:09.999 --> 00:02:10.999
argued about.

00:02:10.999 --> 00:02:11.999
We just find them.

00:02:11.999 --> 00:02:13.129
We can devote more time.

00:02:13.129 --> 00:02:18.390
Thanks to the Internet and the ready availability
of facts to thinking about the implications

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.650
of those facts or the future of humanity,
how we should interpret that information or

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:28.739
whatever the case may be, it feels intuitively
like that's not what's going on.

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:33.069
But what do you think about that idea anyway?

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:36.730
I think, you know, I think it does go on sometimes,
really.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:37.730
Okay.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.749
There are there are good uses of the Internet
and not so good.

00:02:39.749 --> 00:02:42.709
But there are a couple of problems with that
that we've learned.

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:45.780
One is that actually having a super abundance
of information available means anybody can

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.819
find support for any kind of belief, even
though even if it's totally wrong.

00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:53.049
So the idea that we'd all share this base
of facts turns out not to be right.

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:54.280
Human nature gets in the way.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.870
And then the other problem with that is I
think when you're distracted all the time,

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:03.680
when you're constantly taking in information,
you don't have time in your own mind to make

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.769
connections and associations between all the
different pieces of information around you.

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:11.739
And it's those associations and connections
that are required to give you the bigger context

00:03:11.739 --> 00:03:13.379
to fit new information into.

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:14.379
And that broadens your thinking.

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:15.379
Deepens your thinking.

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:16.379
So.

00:03:16.379 --> 00:03:17.540
So, yeah, we can Google all sorts of stuff
on our phones or on our computers, but we're

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.239
losing our ability to fit new information
into a bigger context.

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:21.510
And as a result, we're just kind of turning
into.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:26.180
I would argue, information gatherers that
don't necessarily have an ability to engage

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:31.230
in a deeper conversation with one another
or in a deeper train of thought within ourselves.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:36.800
There's some interesting articles I'm reminded
of about this idea of the political hobbyist,

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.159
which seems very much related to what you're
talking about, which is given so much free

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:47.670
access to information about what's taking
place in the world politically.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:50.160
Comments from elected officials.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.969
A lot of data, so to speak.

00:03:51.969 --> 00:03:55.489
There's arguably a less effective political
activist that comes out of it because they

00:03:55.489 --> 00:03:57.939
are just consuming information, sort of as
you're pointing out, and not actually taking

00:03:57.939 --> 00:04:00.139
that information and doing something with
it to politic, political differences aside,

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.549
to try to influence sort of their community
and their surroundings.

00:04:02.549 --> 00:04:06.090
Is that sort of the idea of what you're saying
when you talk about information overload,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:07.090
which may not be particularly useful?

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:08.150
Yeah, that's that's definitely part of it.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:13.870
I mean, what we know about people is that
if you give them an unlimited amount of information,

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:18.269
they'll go out and cherry pick the information
that reinforces their existing biases.

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:22.669
Whether those biases are based on fact or
fantasy or fiction or whatever.

00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:28.849
And so what happened to our hope when we tell
him we started out with the Net, with the

00:04:28.849 --> 00:04:35.660
Web, was that we'd have such a broad array
of information that people would go out and

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:36.840
sample different opinions, different points
of view.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.419
As you say, become better able to hear other
points, other people and influence them or

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:40.419
be influenced by them.

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:42.120
But what we found is that that rarely happens,
unfortunately.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.620
What what what does happen is we all go out
in our little bubbles in it and gather the

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.490
information that supports our existing biases,
which ends up making those biases even stronger

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:49.919
than we find a lot of people that share those
biases.

00:04:49.919 --> 00:04:55.819
And that has a lot to do with the polarization
we see today politically and socially, and

00:04:55.819 --> 00:04:59.292
an inability to talk beyond the group of people
that that shares our point of view to use

00:04:59.292 --> 00:05:00.660
this sort of economic term there.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.550
There's arguably also a diminishing marginal
utility to information for decision making

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.080
purposes in the sense of, you know, if you're
buying a car as an example, that first information

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.180
that you obtain about, you know, you probably
have a budget.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.180
Right.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.180
So the price of cars is very important.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.180
Do you need a two or.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:11.180
Board or car or a sedan or an SUV.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.180
Over time, the additional information seems
to be less and less useful to actually getting

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.180
to a decision.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.180
Right when you get to.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.180
What are the spark plugs?

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.180
What's the life of the of the LCD bulbs or
whatever?

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.180
And maybe that's also an idea to consider
here.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:18.180
Yeah.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.180
I think I think we're definitely onto something.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.180
And I hadn't really considered that.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:24.639
But, you know, and what happens, I think then
is one of two things.

00:05:24.639 --> 00:05:28.421
Either you become so obsessed by the details
yet that you kind of you.

00:05:28.421 --> 00:05:29.610
You spend all your time gathering more and
more.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.920
Less and less valuable information, and it
prevents you from actually acting because

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:32.920
you suffer information paralysis.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:33.920
And then the other thing is the degree, the
deeper you dig.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.470
If we're talking about political speech, the
more reasons you find to to put other people

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:37.090
at a distance, the more points of difference
you start to uncover, even if they're trivial

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:38.090
points.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:39.090
They loom large as you gather.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.090
They'll keep gathering more and more information
without without necessarily.

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:41.660
As I said, fitting that information and understanding
that information as a part of a bigger, broader

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:42.660
context.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.650
Yeah, that's something I've thought of in
thinking about, for example, like the political

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:47.710
divisions within the left.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:48.710
Where?

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:49.710
Sure.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:50.710
You have some on the left who are almost moderate
Republicans.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:51.710
As an example.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:52.849
And you have some on the left who are truly
socialists.

00:05:52.849 --> 00:05:56.789
But really, there's probably, you know, pick
a number 85, 90.

00:05:56.789 --> 00:06:02.020
I don't know what it would be, but there's
a lot of overlap about sort of what the majority

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:05.860
of those who consider themselves on the mainstream
American political left would want.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:11.270
And yet, when you look at the discourse on
places like Twitter and others, it seems overwhelmingly

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.229
focused on the relatively small differences.

00:06:13.229 --> 00:06:14.229
Yeah.

00:06:14.229 --> 00:06:15.229
You know, in some ways that's nothing new.

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:16.849
People people have been, you know, subdividing
themselves, even with it, even when they share

00:06:16.849 --> 00:06:18.210
a broad understand for a long time.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.280
But I do think the Internet accelerates or
exaggerates that already common occurrence.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.409
It provides not only all sorts of ways to
find new points of distinction, but to to

00:06:24.409 --> 00:06:30.810
find ever narrower groups that share your
precise interests and your in your precise

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:31.810
views.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:32.810
And as soon as you have that group formation,
then even other groups who share a lot of

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:34.220
your views seem like outsiders seem like the
enemy.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:40.550
And I do think that's part of the fragmentation
we see even within within groups, groups of

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:44.470
people who you would think would focus on
their points of agreement to go back where

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:48.419
we started about the effects of the Internet
on our brains and deep thinking, et cetera.

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:51.949
When I've interviewed I interviewed Cal Newport
previously and read some of his books, and

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:56.520
he, at least to some degree, believes that
these are issues of sort of training and thinking

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:59.060
of the brain like a muscle, and that if you
condition the brain to you know, you really

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.259
you close a room and you just read 30 straight
minutes that you're building up a muscle essentially,

00:07:02.259 --> 00:07:04.370
and that this helps to push back against the
instincts that may be pushed on us by sort

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:06.639
of the status quo of Western technology and
the Internet.

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:11.389
Is your take that the solution is a systemic
one, that it's at the individual level, the

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:12.860
absent one, it has to be the other or what?

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:13.860
What's your thought on that?

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:16.189
I certainly think it's I certainly think at
this point we've created a set of social norms

00:07:16.189 --> 00:07:18.900
and social expectations that that essentially
tell us we should always be online, always

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.730
monitoring new information, always sending
out or receiving messages.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:21.880
You get that from employers.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:23.030
You get that from schools.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:24.490
You get that certainly from your friends,
your relations.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:26.330
So I think it's a systemic issue at this point.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:27.850
And that doesn't that doesn't mean there's
not an individual component.

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:29.500
And I think Cowen, Cal Newport's analogy to
building, you know, building muscle is is

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:30.500
the right one.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:31.904
What we know about neuroplasticity plasticity
these days, the brain's malleability, is that

00:07:31.904 --> 00:07:33.930
you really do have to exercise ways of thinking
if you want to retain them.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:34.930
Otherwise, they're going to weaken over time
and and ultimately disappear.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:35.930
And I think as individuals, for a long time
now, we've been training ourselves to gather

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:39.570
information very, very quickly, to pull in
bits of data in lots of overlapping forms.

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.009
We're getting good at that.

00:07:41.009 --> 00:07:42.439
But we have starved ourselves in effect or
not exercised the ability to pay attention,

00:07:42.439 --> 00:07:43.639
which is hard for us to do, as I as I said.

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:44.830
So I think I think if we want to change.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:45.909
You certainly have to pay attention to individual
habits.

00:07:45.909 --> 00:07:47.199
But we also have to recognize that society
is sending these very powerful messages that

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:49.090
tell all of us, stay online, gather as much
information as you can, and don't turn off

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.090
the flow of information.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:54.849
And in practice, the more attentive or contemplative
ways of thinking that are possible for your

00:07:54.849 --> 00:07:56.949
mind, but don't come naturally to us.

00:07:56.949 --> 00:08:03.509
We've been speaking with Nicholas Carr and
we're going to go to a break on the podcast,

00:08:03.509 --> 00:08:04.569
TV and radio show.

00:08:04.569 --> 00:08:06.530
You can find out our full conversation, which
will continue on the YouTube channel,

